From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 7 Jul 2026 05:31:30 -0400
Subject: [RFC PATCH 1/2] firmware: arm_scmi: activate firmware-implemented
 protocols on ACPI

On DT the SCMI node carries one child node per protocol, each with a
"reg" property, and scmi_probe() activates protocols by walking those
children and calling scmi_create_protocol_devices() for each.

On ACPI there is (in practice) no per-protocol child object under the
SCMI device: the ACPI description names the SCMI transport, but the set
of protocols is discovered at runtime from the firmware, not from the
namespace. As a result the child-node loop in scmi_probe() activates
nothing, no protocol devices are created, and every consumer driver
(clocks, perf, power domains, hwmon, reset, ...) fails to bind because
its backing SCMI protocol device never appears.

After the DT child scan, when the SCMI fwnode is an ACPI node, activate
every protocol in the 8-bit id space that the firmware reports as
implemented via BASE_DISCOVER_LIST_PROTOCOLS (scmi_is_protocol_implemented()).
Do NOT probe ids the firmware does not implement: on at least one SCP
implementation, issuing traffic to an unimplemented protocol wedges the
firmware and forces a full SoC reset. Restricting activation to the
firmware-advertised set avoids that.

This is deliberately minimal and self-contained: the DT path is untouched
(the new block only runs for is_acpi_node(np)), and it reuses the existing
protocol-discovery and device-creation helpers.

Note: as written this patch creates the protocol devices but does not yet
set up each protocol's Tx/Rx channel; without that, transfers on the
activated protocols fail. The channel setup is added in patch 2/2 of this
series. The two are split for review clarity but should land together.

Not-yet-Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c b/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c
index 9477e4d9b0c5..b38d76461f39 100644
--- a/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/arm_scmi/driver.c
@@ -3366,6 +3366,35 @@ static int scmi_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		scmi_create_protocol_devices(child, info, prot_id, NULL);
 	}

+	/*
+	 * ACPI: the SCMI node has no protocol child nodes carrying a
+	 * "reg" property, so the loop above activates nothing.  Fall
+	 * back to activating every protocol the firmware reports as
+	 * implemented so consumer drivers (clocks, perf, genpd, hwmon,
+	 * reset) get their scmi devices.
+	 */
+	if (is_acpi_node(np)) {
+		int prot_id;
+
+		for (prot_id = SCMI_PROTOCOL_BASE + 1;
+		     prot_id <= 0xFF; prot_id++) {
+			if (!scmi_is_protocol_implemented(handle, prot_id))
+				continue;
+			if (idr_find(&info->active_protocols, prot_id))
+				continue;
+
+			ret = idr_alloc(&info->active_protocols, (void *)np,
+					prot_id, prot_id + 1, GFP_KERNEL);
+			if (ret != prot_id)
+				continue;
+
+			fwnode_handle_get(np);
+			dev_info(dev, "ACPI: activating SCMI protocol 0x%x\n",
+				 prot_id);
+			scmi_create_protocol_devices(np, info, prot_id, NULL);
+		}
+	}
+
 	return 0;

 notification_exit:
